    <view x="230" wrappingAllowedX="true" y="10" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="0" height="405" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="20.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="true" width="405" tickCounterLabel="ticks" maxPycor="19" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="19" minPxcor="0"></view>
    <button x="10" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="100">setup</button>
    <button x="115" y="10" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="100">go</button>
    <chooser x="10" y="60" height="60" variable="mechanism" current="0" width="205" display="mechanism">
      <choice type="string" value="clumped"></choice>
      <choice type="string" value="variable habitat"></choice>
    </chooser>
    <slider x="10" step="0.5" y="130" max="10.0" width="205" display="mean-per-quadrat" height="50" min="0.5" direction="Horizontal" default="3.0" variable="mean-per-quadrat"></slider>
    <slider x="10" step="1.0" y="185" max="25.0" width="205" display="covey-size" height="50" min="1.0" direction="Horizontal" default="12.0" variable="covey-size"></slider>
    <slider x="10" step="0.1" y="240" max="2.0" width="205" display="habitat-variation" height="50" min="0.0" direction="Horizontal" default="1.0" variable="habitat-variation"></slider>
    <monitor x="10" precision="0" y="300" height="60" fontSize="11" width="205" display="birds landed">placed</monitor>
    <monitor x="10" precision="2" y="365" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="mean">mean-count</monitor>
    <monitor x="115" precision="2" y="365" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="variance / mean">var-to-mean</monitor>
    <monitor x="10" precision="2" y="430" height="60" fontSize="11" width="205" display="fitted size">fitted-size</monitor>
    <plot x="645" autoPlotX="false" yMax="100.0" autoPlotY="true" yAxis="quadrats" y="10" xMin="0.0" height="300" legend="true" xMax="10.0" yMin="0.0" width="430" xAxis="birds in quadrat" display="birds per quadrat">
      <setup></setup>
      <update></update>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="1" display="observed" color="-7500403" legend="true"><setup></setup><update></update></pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="Poisson" color="-2674135" legend="true"><setup></setup><update></update></pen>
      <pen interval="1.0" mode="0" display="negative binomial" color="-13345367" legend="true"><setup></setup><update></update></pen>
    </plot>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

Birds land on a field divided into 400 quadrats, and you count how many end up in each one. That count is the kind of number ecologists collect constantly: birds per survey route, seeds per quadrat, parasites per host.

If every bird lands independently and anywhere, the counts follow a **Poisson** distribution, whose variance equals its mean. Real counts almost never do. They are **clumped**: lots of empty quadrats, and a few with far more birds than a Poisson allows. That pattern is the **negative binomial**.

This model shows two different biological reasons counts end up clumped.

## HOW IT WORKS

Choose a **mechanism**:

- **clumped**: birds arrive in coveys. Each covey picks a random spot and lands there together, scattered a little around it. Use **covey-size** to set how many birds travel together.
- **variable habitat**: birds arrive one at a time and land independently, but quadrats differ in quality, and better quadrats attract more birds. Use **habitat-variation** to set how different the quadrats are.

**mean-per-quadrat** sets how many birds land in total, on average per quadrat.

## HOW TO USE IT

Press **setup**, then **go**. Watch the field fill in, and watch the histogram on the right build up as birds land.

The grey bars are what happened. The red line is what a Poisson with the same mean predicts. The blue line is a negative binomial fitted to the same counts.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

**Variance / mean** is the number to watch. For a Poisson it is 1.

With **covey-size at 1**, every bird lands on its own, and the counts are Poisson: variance / mean sits near 1 and the red line fits.

With **habitat-variation at 0**, every quadrat is equally good, and again the counts are Poisson.

Raise either one and variance / mean climbs well above 1. The red line stops fitting. The blue one does.

**The important part: two different mechanisms, nearly the same counts.** Run clumped with a covey size of 12, then variable habitat with a variation of 1.4. Variance / mean comes out near 7 for both, and the blue negative binomial line fits both histograms reasonably well. From variance / mean alone, you cannot tell which mechanism made the counts.

**Now look at the zero bar.** Under variable habitat it sits right on the blue line: varying habitat is exactly how a negative binomial arises. Under clumping it sticks up above the line. Coveys leave about 200 quadrats empty, where a negative binomial with the same mean and variance predicts about 150.

## THINGS TO TRY

- Find a covey size and a habitat variation that give the same variance / mean. Can you tell the two fields apart by looking at the field? By the histogram? Which bar gives it away?
- Turn mean-per-quadrat down to 1. What happens to the number of empty quadrats under each mechanism?
- The Florida bobwhite counts used in this course have a variance / mean of about 10. From variance / mean alone, you cannot tell which mechanism produced that. But the counts come from the Breeding Bird Survey, run in June. What are bobwhite doing in June? Which mechanism does that rule out, and would your answer change for a winter count?

## CONNECTION TO THE COURSE

Both mechanisms are real for bobwhite, but not at the same time of year. In fall and winter bobwhite live in coveys of roughly 10 to 15 birds. In spring the coveys break up and the birds pair off to breed.

The Florida counts in this course come from the Breeding Bird Survey, which is run in June. Coveys have broken apart by then, so they cannot explain the clumping in those counts. Patchy habitat can: bobwhite need open grassland, old fields and fire-maintained pine, which are scattered across the state, so some routes have many birds and others none. A winter count of the same birds could show both mechanisms at once.

That is a lesson in itself: knowing **when and how the data were collected** can rule out an explanation that the numbers alone cannot. The shape of the distribution, especially the number of zeros, carries a little information too, but with real sample sizes, and two mechanisms possibly acting at once, that signal is weak. Telling them apart usually needs **different data**: watching whether birds arrive together, or measuring habitat directly. That is a general point about models. A good fit to one kind of data can be nearly as good for several different explanations.

The **fitted size** monitor is the negative binomial's size parameter. Small values mean strong clumping. It grows without limit as the counts approach a Poisson.

Supplement 2, *Where distributions come from*, PCB 6455 / BSC 4933: Biometry.
]]></info>
